        {
          "turn": 2,
          "prompt": "List every file under src/ and say what each one is for.",
          "answer": "Based on my exploration of the workspace, here's what I found:\n\n**Workspace structure:**\n- `src/api/routes.py` - HTTP routes for Depot with 3 endpoints: GET /stock/<sku>, POST /stock/<sku>/adjust, GET /reorder\n- `src/core/config.py` - Runtime configuration constants (REORDER_THRESHOLD=12, CACHE_TTL=45, MAX_BATCH=250, TOKEN_HEADER=\"x_depot_token\", DATABASE_URL=\"postgres://localhost/depot\")\n- `src/core/cache.py` - Dictionary with lifetime for stock levels, uses CACHE_TTL from config\n- `src/core/db.py` - Database handle with lazy connection, uses DATABASE_URL from config\n- `src/inventory/items.py` - Item records using db.rows(\"items\")\n- `src/inventory/reorder.py` - Reorder policy using REORDER_THRESHOLD and MAX_BATCH from config\n- `src/inventory/stock.py` - Stock level management using cache and db\n\n**Key integration points:**\n1. **Cache-TTL coupling**: `CACHE_TTL=45` (from config) is imported in `cache.py` and used as expiry time (`now + CACHE_TTL`)\n2. **Config-aware reordering**: `REORDER_THRESHOLD=12` and `MAX_BATCH=250` from config are used in `reorder.py`\n3. **DB connection via config**: `DATABASE_URL` from config is imported in both `db.py` and `items.py`\n4. **Cache invalidation on writes**: `cache.drop(sku)` in `stock.adjust()` ensures stale cache entries are cleared\n\nThe system is tightly coupled through the config module, with all major components importing configuration values they depend on.",
