        {
          "turn": 3,
          "prompt": "Read src/api/routes.py and explain what each route does.",
          "answer": "Based on reading `src/api/routes.py`, here are the three HTTP routes:\n\n**1. GET /stock/<sku>**\n- Decorated with `@require_token`\n- Takes an SKU from the request path\n- Calls `level_for(request.sku)` to get the current stock level for that item\n- Returns `{\"sku\": <sku>, \"level\": <current_level>}`\n\n**2. POST /stock/<sku>/adjust**\n- Decorated with `@require_token`\n- Takes an SKU from the request path and a `delta` value from the request body\n- Calls `adjust(request.sku, request.delta)` to move the stock level up or down by delta\n- Returns `{\"sku\": <sku>, \"level\": <new_level>}` after adjustment\n\n**3. GET /reorder**\n- Decorated with `@require_token`\n- No path parameters needed\n- Calls `reorder_due()` which queries all items and returns those at or below the `REORDER_THRESHOLD` (from config)\n- Returns `{\"due\": [<list of SKUs needing reorder>]}`\n\nAll three routes share the `@require_token` dependency, meaning each endpoint requires valid authentication before it can be accessed.",
